2.0.0 (unreleased)
  - A login attempt is refused (503, "try again in a few minutes") if the
    block list can't be read; it used to count as not blocked. Other
    requests go on as before
  - Parallel failed logins of one client which all reach the threshold
    block it once: each used to find it not blocked yet and block it, so a
    single burst counted as several earlier offences (making the block
    permanent with "Permanent block after", or multiplying a progressive
    duration) and sent a mail for each. The decision is now made under a
    lock per tracked address
  - Security: no .htaccess block of a network with allowed addresses in it.
    The web server knows nothing of the allow list: an IPv6 client is
    blocked by its /56, and in .htaccess that turned away the allowed hosts
    of the network too (it is now only blocked in the database, like a
    trusted proxy). The backend's .htaccess form refuses an address or
    network which overlaps the allow list - it only refused addresses which
    were on it, and never noticed one for a network
  - Security: no addresses taken from the request in mails. Without the
    site's $live_site, Joomla builds every address from the host the request
    names, and the mails are prepared in the request of a failed login -
    whoever sends it picks the host. Unblock links are therefore only sent
    with $live_site set, also in "only if the user has logged in from that
    IP address before" mode: other people may share the address (a NAT, an
    office, an IPv6 network) and could get a genuine mail of the site sent
    to the user with a link to their own server. The settings page doesn't
    take unblock links without $live_site; the notifications to the
    administrators name no address without it
  - Security: blocking through .htaccess no longer opens up the directory it
    writes into. The block section of earlier versions held "Require all
    granted", which Apache combines with the directory's other access
    controls by "any of them is enough": everybody who wasn't blocked got
    past HTTP authentication, "Require ip" and "Require all denied" in that
    directory, and a blocked client got in with valid credentials. The
    section now only denies (<If "-R '...'"> Require all denied </If>); the
    update replaces the section of earlier versions (and says so if it
    can't write the file), and the daily maintenance does as well. A CI job
    checks the section against a real Apache
  - .htaccess files with Windows line ends: their blocks are listed and
    removed again (they used to stay in the file for ever)
  - Migrate to Joomla 5/6: PSR-4 namespaced classes (Codeling\Plugin\System\Bfstop),
    service provider, SubscriberInterface event handling
  - Drop Joomla 3/4 compatibility (requires Joomla 5 or 6 and PHP 8.1); the
    installer refuses older Joomla and PHP versions, and replacing a newer
    version of BFStop with an older one
  - Component: PSR-4 namespaced classes (Codeling\Component\Bfstop); the
    frontend router.php was removed (all of its build/parse logic was
    commented out), Joomla's default component router is used instead, with
    no change in behaviour
  - Modernize token generation to use random_bytes()/random_int()
  - Consolidate all configuration into the component's Settings view
    (Components -> Brute Force Stop -> Settings); the plugin's Options tab
    and description now just point there instead of holding real settings
  - Adaptive allowance of failed attempts (#76): failed logins for a
    username are now also throttled across all source IPs combined, not
    just per IP, so a distributed attack spreading attempts against one
    account across many IPs can no longer evade the per-IP threshold; a
    new per-attempt risk score (known IP/username pairs, common usernames,
    missing User-Agent, optional GeoIP country and reverse-DNS signals)
    adjusts the delay and allowed-attempts threshold; optional progressive
    (exponential) block duration for repeat offenders; password reset/
    username reminder now stay reachable even for a blocked IP
  - Notifications of failed logins only for existing accounts (#172): the new
    setting "Failed msgs only for existing accounts" restricts the failed
    login notification to attempts with a username or email address which
    belongs to a registered account; attempts with other names are still
    recorded and counted towards blocking
  - Local GeoIP lookup (issue #169): "Information for IP Address" in the
    component now shows real country/region/city/coordinates again via a
    locally-configured MaxMind .mmdb database, instead of the discontinued
    freegeoip.net API it used to call
  - Username statistics (#136): the number of failed logins per username,
    with first and last attempt, is now kept in a separate table which is
    not affected by the automatic purge of old failed login entries; it is
    seeded from the retained failed login entries on update, and shown as
    a "highscore" of attacked usernames in the component
  - All BFStop tables now use the utf8mb4 character set (like Joomla's own
    tables), so failed logins for usernames containing 4-byte characters
    (e.g. emoji) are recorded too, instead of failing to be stored
  - Manual cleanup of failed login entries (#214): the component's failed
    login list has a "Delete Old Entries" button, which asks for the age (in
    days) of the entries to keep and deletes all older ones (blocks are not
    affected), and a hint pointing at the setting for automatically deleting
    old entries
  - Not implemented (documented as future work): CAPTCHA/challenge-based
    responses and IP-reputation/hosting-vs-residential classification were
    both considered but require either login-page changes or an
    additional external database dependency; a real risk-based
    step-up-authentication flow (e.g. requiring Joomla's built-in
    two-factor auth when risk is high) would need to hook earlier in the
    login pipeline than this plugin currently does and is left for a
    separate, larger initiative
  - PostgreSQL support (#206): install/uninstall scripts for PostgreSQL;
    the plugin previously installed without creating its tables there and
    then silently never blocked anything. IP subnet matching for block and
    allow list entries is now done in PHP instead of with MySQL-only SQL
    functions, and fixed-length time windows are computed in PHP
  - Security: an emailed unblock token no longer lets a blocked IP address
    bypass the block. It was accepted on any request with
    view=tokenunblock (e.g. a login), was never used up by such a request
    and was not checked for expiry, so anyone who got a token (e.g. by
    getting their own account blocked) could keep guessing passwords from
    a blocked IP. Now it only passes the unblock page of com_bfstop itself,
    only while unexpired, and only if issued for a block of the requesting
    IP address; the token is no longer written to the log either
  - Security: behind a proxy/load balancer, the client IP is now taken from
    the end of the forwarding header (the entry added by the trusted proxy)
    instead of its first public entry, which a visitor could forge to evade
    blocking, get other addresses blocked or impersonate an allowlisted one.
    Several trusted proxies (and CIDR subnets) can be configured, the
    "Forwarded" header (RFC 7239) and addresses with ports are understood,
    and private client addresses reported by the proxy are accepted. Not
    all proxies append to an incoming header: pick a header yours sets or
    appends to
  - Security: a failed login is now recorded, counted and checked against
    the block threshold before the configured delay is applied, not after
    it, and a request whose IP address just got (or already is) blocked is
    not delayed any more. Before, a burst of parallel login attempts all
    ran before the first of them was counted, and every one of them held
    on to a server worker for the whole delay
  - Security: usernames of failed logins which do not belong to an existing
    account (and are not on the list of common usernames) are now stored,
    shown, logged and mailed as an unreadable hash by default, as users
    often type their password into the username field; the hash is the same
    for the same input, so attempts are still counted together. The new
    setting "Usernames Not Matching an Account" restores the old behaviour.
    Entries stored by earlier versions stay as they are - purge them in the
    failed login and username statistics views if you want them gone
  - Security: unblocking by the link in the notification email now needs a
    confirmation (a POST request) and only works from the IP address which
    was blocked, so mail security scanners or link previews which open
    every link in a message can't unblock an attacker (see com_bfstop)
  - Security: the "User Block Message" with the unblock link is no longer
    sent if the failed logins from the blocked IP address which led to the
    block also went against other existing accounts. The link goes to the
    owner of the account of the last attempt, so an attacker with an account
    of their own could otherwise end every series of guesses at other
    accounts with an attempt at their own, get the link and continue.
    Attempts against names which don't exist (e.g. typos) don't count
  - Security: anybody can make the plugin send the "User Block Message" to
    a user, by failing to log in with that username from an address until it
    is blocked - a way to flood somebody's inbox. The setting now has three
    values: disabled; enabled only if the user has logged in from the
    blocked IP address (or, for IPv6, its network) before, which is the
    case for somebody locking themselves out but never for an attacker's
    address; and enabled for any IP address, where a user gets at most one
    unblock link at a time which can still be used (not yet expired or used
    up). Note that the previous "enabled" now means the first of these; the
    old behaviour is the third. Known IP addresses are recorded at
    successful logins, so users get the message again after having logged
    in once. The username a link was sent to is recorded with its token
  - The addresses users logged in from (used for the risk score and for the
    unblock message) are remembered by network for IPv6 clients, as failed
    logins are, so a client whose address changes within its /64 every day
    stays a known one and doesn't add a row per address; the lookup ignores
    the case of the username. They are forgotten a year after the last login
    from them (and the table is limited to 100000 rows, oldest first). This
    maintenance, like the limit of the username statistics, runs once a day
    whatever the purge age is set to
  - Security: the cached results of the reverse-DNS risk signal are deleted
    by the daily maintenance once they have expired, whatever the purge age
    is set to (before: only by the purge by age, i.e. never with a purge age
    of 0), and limited to 10000 rows, oldest first: the cache has a row per
    address, and an IPv6 client can fail every login from another one
  - Unblock tokens which have expired are deleted by the daily maintenance too
    (before: only when somebody visited the unblock page, or by the purge by
    age if one is configured). The status code of the block page is set with
    http_response_code() instead of a raw "HTTP/1.0 403" header, which hard-coded
    the protocol version
  - The unblock page of com_bfstop answers with an error status if the link
    can't be used (400 without a token, 403 from another IP address, 404 for
    an unknown, expired or used token, 500 if unblocking failed), unless "Use
    HTTP Error" is switched off; opening the link (a GET request) stays a 200
    for every token. The page is not cached and tells browsers not to send
    its address (which contains the token) to other sites and search engines
    not to index it
  - Security: IPv6 clients are now tracked per network (default: /56, see
    the new "IPv6 Tracking Granularity" setting) instead of per address:
    failed logins are counted and blocks apply to the whole network, so an
    attacker can no longer start over by switching to another address in
    their network (a connection usually gets a /56, i.e. 256 /64s). IPv4-mapped, NAT64, 6to4 and Teredo addresses, which embed
    unrelated clients' IPv4 addresses, are still tracked individually
  - Security: the block page is never cached (Cache-Control: no-store) and,
    if no setting says otherwise, answers with the HTTP status 403 instead of
    200, so a caching proxy/CDN can't serve it to other visitors
  - Security: only a valid IP address or subnet is written to the .htaccess
    file (before, a value like "192.0.2.0/1e1" passed the backend validation
    and made Apache reject the whole file); the 403 message is escaped; and
    changes of the file are done while holding a lock, so that clients
    getting blocked at the same time don't overwrite each other's lines
  - Log file (#185): besides being rotated once it exceeds a configurable size
    (default 5 MB), entries older than a configurable number of days (default
    28) are now deleted from it once a day, also when logging was disabled in
    the meantime; the log files are removed when the plugin is uninstalled, and
    the component's log view got buttons to prune or clear the log
  - Security: the plugin's log file is rotated at 5 MB, and the username
    statistics (which aren't purged by age) are limited to 10000 usernames,
    removing those with the fewest attempts first, so a visitor can't fill up
    disk or database by making up usernames
  - Security: the periodic maintenance (once a day) only updates its own
    timestamp in the plugin's stored settings, instead of writing back all
    settings as they were when the request started, which could undo a
    change an administrator saved in the meantime; and it now also runs if
    no purge age is configured
  - Security (component): sending the test email now needs the permission to
    change the settings; the .htaccess path and GeoIP database path settings
    are validated (no stream wrappers like phar://, a .mmdb file name)
  - Added SECURITY.md with the supported versions and how to report
    vulnerabilities
  - Security: the check of each request against the block and allow list is
    a lookup by index. It used to read all networks of both lists and test
    each in PHP, for every request of every visitor: 65-70 ms with 170000
    entries, now 1-2 ms. Addresses and networks are stored in a canonical
    form (lower case, compressed, networks as their network address, like
    "2001:db8:1::/48"); the component's forms do that, and the plugin
    converts existing entries once, on the first request after the update.
    Entries which are added to the tables by other means have to be in that
    form as well
  - Security: the failed logins are indexed by address and by time. With
    the 200000 of them that are kept at most, the lookups made for every
    failed login took 9-27 ms each (PostgreSQL 16 / MariaDB 10.11), the one
    made when an address gets blocked up to 190 ms on MariaDB, and those of
    the adaptive delay 8-18 ms; now each takes below 1 ms, while recording
    a failed login takes as long as before
  - Security: the bundled MaxMind DB reader (maxmind-db/reader, now 1.14.0,
    which limits the decoding of malformed or hostile database files) is
    tracked in composer.json/composer.lock; src/Helper/Geo is generated from
    it (tools/vendor-geoip.php), CI verifies that, and Dependabot plus a
    weekly job report new releases and security advisories
  - Security: blocking through .htaccess removes blocks that ran out or were
    lifted from the file (a timed block used to stay there for ever, and the
    file grew with every blocked address); at most 2000 lines are kept; the
    file is replaced atomically instead of being rewritten in place; the lock
    file lives in Joomla's tmp directory instead of the system's. A block
    lifted in the component's block list or with an unblock link is taken out
    of the file right away, not when the plugin next blocks an address or
    does its daily maintenance (until then, the web server kept rejecting it)
  - Security: in "Login Only" block mode, a blocked address could still try
    passwords at the backend's login by leaving "option=com_login" out of
    the request, or naming any other component: Joomla's backend runs
    com_login for every request of a guest. Such requests are recognised as
    the logins they are now; the exemptions for password recovery and the
    unblock page don't let a blocked address through to the backend either
  - Security: logins through Joomla's Web Services API (api/index.php, with
    an API token or HTTP Basic authentication) are protected as well. Before,
    the plugin never looked at the API, so passwords could be tried there
    without being counted, and blocked addresses could still use it. Now
    failed API logins are counted (those of requests which carry credentials:
    Joomla also reports one for every request to an endpoint which isn't
    public, and a request without credentials guesses nothing), and a blocked
    address gets an error from the API ("Login Only" mode: every request with
    credentials is a login). "Active for" has the API as a third part besides
    frontend and backend, with a checkbox for each part (instead of a list of
    their combinations); it is on by default, an update from 1.x adds it to
    a saved setting, and the update says so. The parts are recognised by the
    application a request runs in (before: by its client id + 1, which would
    have made Joomla's installation and console applications look like other
    parts). The description of the setting
    no longer claims that a block always applies to the whole site: blocks
    have only ever been enforced in the parts the plugin is active for
    (blocking through .htaccess aside)
  - Component: unblocking several entries of the block list at once no
    longer stops at the first one which was unblocked already (the others
    stayed blocked), and a database error while unblocking is reported as a
    failure; the allow list can be sorted by its columns again; blocks of a
    week, 30 days or a duration of the progressive block duration show their
    duration instead of a language key
  - Security: failed logins are purged after 4 weeks by default (set the
    purge age to 0 for the old behaviour) and limited to 200000 rows
  - Security: a response is delayed by 60 seconds at most, however the delay
    settings add up, and the account throttle no longer delays an address
    that was just blocked. "Delay Duration" and "Maximum Adaptive Delay" go
    up to 60 seconds (before: 120); a larger value saved by an earlier
    version has to be lowered the next time the settings are saved
  - Security: unblock tokens are stored as hashes; links sent before the
    update stop working (they are valid for 3 days only)
  - Security: usernames in log entries and in mails can no longer contain
    line breaks; array-valued request parameters ("option[]=x") no longer
    cause a TypeError in the check of blocked requests
  - Settings which have not been saved yet now have the values the settings
    page shows (block after 10 attempts, ...)
  - Component: all list settings are validated against their options, the
    notification addresses against the email format; messages escape what was
    typed in; the settings and log views need the core.admin permission
  - Internal: the Joomla! APIs deprecated in 4.3-5.3 and removed in Joomla! 7
    are no longer used (Factory::getDbo/getConfig/getSession/getLanguage/
    getDocument/getMailer, $_db, Table::getInstance, View::get(),
    addStyleSheet(), getCfg()); a test fails if one comes back
  - Translations: the strings added since 0.9 are now translated into all
    languages (the language check reports no missing keys any more);
    Portuguese (Brazil and Portugal) is complete and is installed with the
    plugin and the component; the installation hint no longer shows markup
    and an empty link when installing from the command line
  - Security: unblock links for any IP address ("User Block Message"
    "for any IP address") need the site's address, $live_site in
    configuration.php. Without it, Joomla builds the link from the host named
    by the request of the failed login which got the address blocked - the
    attacker's - so a link to the attacker's server (opened by mail scanners)
    could hand them the token for unblocking their own address, again and
    again. Without $live_site, such links are only sent where "only if the
    user has logged in from that IP address before" sends them; the settings
    page doesn't take the setting then and says what to add to
    configuration.php, and the backend warns about a setting saved before
  - Security: delays can no longer be used to run the server out of PHP
    workers. A delayed response holds a worker for the whole delay, and an
    attacker with many addresses (an IPv6 network gives plenty) could send
    failed logins in parallel until none was left. At most "Responses
    delayed at the same time" (default 5) responses are delayed at once;
    further ones are answered without delay, and still counted and blocked.
    The risk score adds no delay by default any more ("Delay Seconds Per
    Risk Point" 0, before: 2, i.e. 4 seconds for a failed login as "admin")
  - Security: the reverse DNS risk signal can no longer hold a request for
    as long as the attacker's name servers like. The lookup is made by the
    plugin over UDP, with the name servers of /etc/resolv.conf, and given up
    on after 1 second (gethostbyaddr() can't be given a timeout, and the
    system resolver retries for up to half a minute); no answer counts as no
    record. Results are cached by the network an IPv6 client is tracked by,
    not by its address, so switching addresses no longer means a new lookup
    with every failed login, and the failed login is recorded before the
    lookup. Without /etc/resolv.conf (e.g. on Windows) the signal is skipped
  - Security (component): clearing and pruning the log need the core.admin
    permission, as viewing it does (before: core.delete only, so a user who
    may only delete entries of the lists could erase the log without being
    able to read it); core.delete is still needed as well
  - Security (component): adding and changing entries of the allow list need
    the core.admin permission besides core.create / core.edit - an entry
    exempts addresses from all protection, as changing the settings could
    (removing entries still needs core.delete only). The block, .htaccess
    block and allow forms no longer take networks larger than /8 (IPv4) or
    /16 (IPv6): an allow list entry like 0.0.0.0/0 switched the protection
    off, and a block of ::/0 entered from an IPv4 address shut out every
    IPv6 visitor without the warning a block of one's own address gets
  - Security: with proxy support on, a trusted proxy is no longer written to
    the .htaccess file. Failed logins through it whose forwarding header
    doesn't name the client are still counted against its address (not
    counting them would let such requests fail logins without limit), and
    blocked in the database - which only holds back requests resolving to
    that address -, but the web server would turn away everybody coming
    through the proxy. The component's .htaccess block form refuses it as
    well. The help of the proxy setting warns against trusting a whole
    address range of a CDN or hosting provider
  - Component: the lists build their ORDER BY from the filter fields only,
    with the column quoted as an identifier (before: escape(), which is for
    string literals, relying on Joomla's check of the request)

